window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"Designing Data-Intensive Applications — Kleppmann's ideas, made visual","section":"Designing Data-Intensive Applications, distilled.","tool":"home","text":"Designing Data-Intensive Applications, distilled. A sharp, crisp tour of Martin Kleppmann's DDIA — not a re-telling, just the ideas that move the needle. Every concept is given twice : a plain-English analogy, then the precise version, with diagrams, trade-off sliders, and an interactive data-model ","keywords":["designing","data","intensive","applications","distilled"]},{"url":"foundations/reliable-scalable-maintainable.html","page":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable","section":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable.","tool":"foundations","text":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable. Before any database or queue, three words decide whether a data system is any good: it should keep working when things go wrong ( reliable ), keep working as it grows ( scalable ), and keep being pleasant to work on ( maintainable ). Everything else in the book is ","keywords":["reliable","scalable","maintainable"]},{"url":"foundations/reliable-scalable-maintainable.html#big-three","page":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable","section":"The three words","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The three words A data-intensive app is limited not by CPU power but by data — its amount, complexity, and rate of change. The job is to keep that data reliable, scalable, and maintainable . In plain English analogy Think of a city's water utility. Reliable = clean water keeps flowing even when a","keywords":["the","three","words"]},{"url":"foundations/reliable-scalable-maintainable.html#reliability","page":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable","section":"Reliability — tolerating faults","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Reliability — tolerating faults Reliability means the system keeps doing the right thing, at acceptable performance, even when things go wrong . The things that go wrong are faults ; a fault that stops the whole system serving users is a failure . You can't prevent faults — you build systems that","keywords":["reliability","tolerating","faults"]},{"url":"foundations/reliable-scalable-maintainable.html#scalability","page":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable","section":"Scalability — describing & handling load","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Scalability — describing & handling load Scalability isn't a label (“X is scalable”) — it's a question: if load grows in a particular way, what are our options for coping? To answer it you must first describe load with numbers, then describe performance honestly. Describe load with a parameter Kl","keywords":["scalability","describing","handling","load"]},{"url":"foundations/reliable-scalable-maintainable.html#maintainability","page":"Reliable, scalable & maintainable","section":"Maintainability — operability, simplicity, evolvability","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Maintainability — operability, simplicity, evolvability The majority of a system's cost is after it ships. Maintainability has three sides: Operability Make it easy for ops to keep it running — good monitoring, predictable behaviour, sane defaults, self-healing where possible. Simplicity Manage c","keywords":["maintainability","operability","simplicity","evolvability"]},{"url":"foundations/data-models.html","page":"Data models & query languages","section":"Data models & query languages.","tool":"foundations","text":"Data models & query languages. The data model is the most important choice you make — it shapes not just how you store data, but how you think about the problem. Three families dominate: relational (tables), document (self-contained trees), and graph (everything connected). None is “best”; each fits","keywords":["data","models","query","languages"]},{"url":"foundations/data-models.html#three-models","page":"Data models & query languages","section":"One profile, three models","tool":"foundations","text":"01 One profile, three models Pick the model that matches the shape of your relationships : one-to-many → document, many-to-one / many-to-many → relational, densely connected → graph. In plain English analogy It's how you organise a library. Relational = shelves by subject with a card catalogue that ","keywords":["one","profile","three","models"]},{"url":"foundations/data-models.html#relational-vs-document","page":"Data models & query languages","section":"Relational vs document","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Relational vs document The old debate, settled pragmatically: it depends on your data's relationships. The friction that pushed people to document stores is the impedance mismatch — the awkward translation layer between relational tables and the nested objects in application code. A document (JSO","keywords":["relational","vs","document"]},{"url":"foundations/data-models.html#schema","page":"Data models & query languages","section":"Schema-on-read vs schema-on-write","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Schema-on-read vs schema-on-write “Schemaless” is misleading — there's always a schema; the question is when it's enforced. Schema-on-read · document Structure is interpreted when you read . Like a dynamically-typed language — flexible, forgiving of heterogeneous or evolving data. Adding a field ","keywords":["schema","on","read","vs","write","ALTER","TABLE","Schema","document","no","migration","old","docs","simply","lack","the","field","if","middle_name","in","user","app","handles","both","shapes","show","relational","change","first","then","backfill","users","ADD","COLUMN","TEXT","UPDATE","SET","split_name","full_name"]},{"url":"foundations/data-models.html#query-languages","page":"Data models & query languages","section":"Declarative vs imperative queries","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Declarative vs imperative queries How you ask for data matters as much as how you store it. Declarative (SQL, Cypher, CSS) You say what you want — “all active users in Seattle” — not how to get it. The engine chooses the execution plan, uses indexes, and can parallelise across cores and machines.","keywords":["declarative","vs","imperative","queries"]},{"url":"foundations/storage-engines.html","page":"Storage & retrieval","section":"Storage & retrieval.","tool":"foundations","text":"Storage & retrieval. A database does two things: when you give it data, store it; when you ask, give it back. How it does that under the hood splits storage engines into two great families — log-structured (LSM-trees) and page-oriented (B-trees) — and the choice quietly decides whether your workload","keywords":["storage","retrieval"]},{"url":"foundations/storage-engines.html#the-log","page":"Storage & retrieval","section":"The simplest database is a log","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The simplest database is a log Appending to a file is the fastest possible write. Every fast storage engine is built on that idea — and then adds an index so reads don't have to scan the whole file. In plain English analogy Two ways to run a stockroom. (1) Scribble every change at the bottom of a","keywords":["the","simplest","database","is","log"]},{"url":"foundations/storage-engines.html#two-families","page":"Storage & retrieval","section":"The two families","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The two families Flip between them. Both keep keys sorted; they differ in where the work happens . How the engine stores & finds data Interactive — needs JavaScript. LSM-trees append to an in-memory memtable then flush sorted SSTables; B-trees update fixed-size pages in place.","keywords":["the","two","families"]},{"url":"foundations/storage-engines.html#oltp-olap","page":"Storage & retrieval","section":"Two worlds: OLTP vs OLAP","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Two worlds: OLTP vs OLAP Storage layout also depends on access pattern . Most apps are OLTP (transaction processing): tiny reads/writes by key, latency-critical. Analytics is OLAP : scan billions of rows over a few columns. They want opposite layouts. Row-oriented · OLTP Store all of a row's colu","keywords":["two","worlds","oltp","vs","olap"]},{"url":"foundations/encoding-evolution.html","page":"Encoding & evolution","section":"Encoding & evolution.","tool":"foundations","text":"Encoding & evolution. Data in memory is pointers and objects; data on disk or a wire is just bytes. Turning one into the other is encoding . Get it right and old and new versions of your code can keep talking to each other through a deploy — get it wrong and a rollout becomes an outage.","keywords":["encoding","evolution"]},{"url":"foundations/encoding-evolution.html#why-encoding","page":"Encoding & evolution","section":"Why encoding exists","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Why encoding exists In-memory objects can't leave the process. To store or send them you encode (serialize) to bytes; to use them again you decode . The format you choose decides size, speed, and — crucially — how gracefully your schema can change. In plain English analogy You can't mail a though","keywords":["why","encoding","exists"]},{"url":"foundations/encoding-evolution.html#formats","page":"Encoding & evolution","section":"Textual vs binary-with-schema","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Textual vs binary-with-schema Textual · JSON / XML / CSV Human-readable and universal. But verbose, ambiguous about numbers (is it an int? how big?), and the field names ship in every record. Great for APIs and config; wasteful at scale. Binary with a schema · Protobuf / Thrift / Avro A schema de","keywords":["textual","vs","binary","with","schema","message","Person","Protobuf","style","string","name","field","tag","its","identity","forever","int32","birth_year","city","NEW","added","later","see","below"]},{"url":"foundations/encoding-evolution.html#evolution","page":"Encoding & evolution","section":"Schema evolution — the whole point","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Schema evolution — the whole point During a rolling deploy, old and new code run at the same time , reading each other's data. So a schema change must keep working in both directions: Backward compatible New code reads old data. Easy — new code knows the old fields. (A new reader just handles a m","keywords":["schema","evolution","the","whole","point"]},{"url":"foundations/encoding-evolution.html#dataflow","page":"Encoding & evolution","section":"Where encoded data flows","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Where encoded data flows Compatibility matters because encoded data crosses boundaries between code versions in three ways: Through databases The writer may be newer than the reader (or vice-versa). Data outlives code — a row written years ago must still decode. Beware: old code that reads + rewr","keywords":["where","encoded","data","flows"]},{"url":"distributed/replication.html","page":"Replication","section":"Replication.","tool":"distributed","text":"Replication. Keep a copy of the same data on more than one machine. Do it well and you get lower latency, higher availability, and more read throughput — almost for free. The whole difficulty is one word: change . When the data is updated, how do all the copies stay in step?","keywords":["replication"]},{"url":"distributed/replication.html#why-replicate","page":"Replication","section":"Why keep copies at all","tool":"distributed","text":"01 Why keep copies at all Replication buys you three things: availability (a node dies, others carry on), latency (a copy near each user), and read throughput (spread reads over many machines). The price you pay is keeping the copies consistent. In plain English analogy One master ledger in head off","keywords":["why","keep","copies","at","all"]},{"url":"distributed/replication.html#leader-model","page":"Replication","section":"The leader-based model","tool":"distributed","text":"02 The leader-based model The most common scheme by far. One replica is the leader (primary/master); the rest are followers (replicas/secondaries). All writes go to the leader. The leader applies the change, then streams it — as a replication log — to every follower, which replays it in the same ord","keywords":["the","leader","based","model"]},{"url":"distributed/replication.html#lag","page":"Replication","section":"Replication lag and the guarantees that hide it","tool":"distributed","text":"03 Replication lag and the guarantees that hide it Asynchronous followers are eventually consistent — given no new writes, they catch up. But “eventually” can be milliseconds or minutes, and in that window users hit confusing anomalies. Three guarantees paper over the three classic ones: Read-your-w","keywords":["replication","lag","and","the","guarantees","that","hide","it"]},{"url":"distributed/replication.html#topologies","page":"Replication","section":"One leader, many leaders, or none","tool":"distributed","text":"04 One leader, many leaders, or none Single-leader is simple but funnels every write through one node — bad across datacenters or offline clients. Two alternatives relax that: Multi-leader A leader per datacenter (or per device). Each accepts writes locally and replicates to the others. Great for mu","keywords":["one","leader","many","leaders","or","none","replicas","per","key","that","must","ack","write","consulted","on","read","def","is_quorum","overlap","guaranteed","when","the","set","and","intersect","return","assert","sees","latest","not","reads","may","miss","recent","writes"]},{"url":"distributed/partitioning.html","page":"Partitioning","section":"Partitioning.","tool":"distributed","text":"Partitioning. Replication copies the whole dataset to every node. But when the data is too big — or too busy — for any single machine, you must split it: each partition (shard) lives on a different node. The art is deciding which record goes where, so that load spreads evenly and growing the cluster","keywords":["partitioning"]},{"url":"distributed/partitioning.html#why-partition","page":"Partitioning","section":"Why split the data","tool":"distributed","text":"01 Why split the data Replication is for availability ; partitioning is for scale . Each partition is a little database of its own — spread them across nodes and both storage and throughput scale with the cluster. Partitioning and replication almost always travel together: each partition is also rep","keywords":["why","split","the","data"]},{"url":"distributed/partitioning.html#range-vs-hash","page":"Partitioning","section":"Two ways to assign keys","tool":"distributed","text":"02 Two ways to assign keys By key range Contiguous ranges of the key, kept sorted (like an encyclopedia's volumes). Range scans are efficient — “all events in March” reads one partition. Risk: an uneven or sequential key (a timestamp) sends all new writes to one partition — a hot spot. By hash of ke","keywords":["two","ways","to","assign","keys"]},{"url":"distributed/partitioning.html#ring","page":"Partitioning","section":"The ring — and why rebalancing is the real problem","tool":"distributed","text":"03 The ring — and why rebalancing is the real problem Hashing fixes skew, but raises a new question: how many partitions, and on which node? The naïve answer node = hash(key) % N is a trap — change N (add or remove a node) and almost every key remaps, moving the entire dataset over the network. Cons","keywords":["the","ring","and","why","rebalancing","is","real","problem","node","hash","key"]},{"url":"distributed/partitioning.html#routing","page":"Partitioning","section":"Finding the data again","tool":"distributed","text":"04 Finding the data again Once data is scattered, a client asking for key k needs to reach the right partition — this is request routing , an instance of service discovery. Three designs: let any node forward the request, put a routing tier in front, or make clients partition-aware. A coordination s","keywords":["finding","the","data","again"]},{"url":"distributed/transactions.html","page":"Transactions","section":"Transactions.","tool":"distributed","text":"Transactions. A transaction groups several reads and writes into one all-or-nothing unit. Its job is to be a safety blanket : it lets the application pretend there are no concurrent users and no partial failures. The whole chapter is about how strong that blanket really is — and what it costs to mak","keywords":["transactions"]},{"url":"distributed/transactions.html#acid","page":"Transactions","section":"What ACID actually promises","tool":"distributed","text":"01 What ACID actually promises A transaction either happens completely or not at all, and runs as if it had the database to itself. That illusion is built from four guarantees — but the word ACID hides as much as it reveals. In plain English analogy A bank transfer is two steps: debit one account, c","keywords":["what","acid","actually","promises"]},{"url":"distributed/transactions.html#anomalies","page":"Transactions","section":"The race conditions isolation must stop","tool":"distributed","text":"02 The race conditions isolation must stop Weak isolation lets concurrent transactions interfere in specific, named ways. Knowing the anomalies is how you pick a level: Dirty read / dirty write Reading another transaction's uncommitted write (dirty read), or overwriting it (dirty write). The floor m","keywords":["the","race","conditions","isolation","must","stop","SELECT","FOR","UPDATE"]},{"url":"distributed/transactions.html#levels","page":"Transactions","section":"Isolation levels — the dial","tool":"distributed","text":"03 Isolation levels — the dial Isolation is a dial from “fast and loose” to “correct and costly.” Most databases default to a weak level for performance, so you must know which anomalies your data can't tolerate.","keywords":["isolation","levels","the","dial"]},{"url":"distributed/transactions.html#serializability","page":"Transactions","section":"Three ways to be serializable","tool":"distributed","text":"04 Three ways to be serializable If serializable is so safe, why isn't it the default? Because every implementation pays for it differently: Actual serial execution Just run one transaction at a time on a single thread (VoltDB, Redis). Trivially correct — viable now that RAM is large and transaction","keywords":["three","ways","to","be","serializable","SERIALIZABLE"]},{"url":"distributed/distributed-trouble.html","page":"The trouble with distributed systems","section":"The trouble with distributed systems.","tool":"distributed","text":"The trouble with distributed systems. On one machine, things are deterministic: an operation works, or the whole machine is down. Spread across a network and that comfort vanishes — messages vanish or arrive late, clocks disagree, and a node can freeze mid-step. This chapter is a catalogue of the wa","keywords":["the","trouble","with","distributed","systems"]},{"url":"distributed/distributed-trouble.html#networks","page":"The trouble with distributed systems","section":"The network is unreliable","tool":"distributed","text":"01 The network is unreliable When you send a request and hear nothing back, you cannot tell whether the node never got it, is still working on it, crashed, or replied and the reply was lost. A timeout is just a guess dressed up as a fact. In plain English analogy You mail a colleague a letter and he","keywords":["the","network","is","unreliable"]},{"url":"distributed/distributed-trouble.html#clocks","page":"The trouble with distributed systems","section":"Clocks lie","tool":"distributed","text":"02 Clocks lie Every machine has clocks, and they drift. Synchronisation (NTP) helps but can't make them trustworthy for ordering events across nodes. Crucially, there are two kinds, and confusing them causes bugs: Time-of-day clock “Wall clock” — seconds since 1970. Can jump backwards on an NTP corr","keywords":["clocks","lie"]},{"url":"distributed/distributed-trouble.html#pauses","page":"The trouble with distributed systems","section":"Processes pause — and the leader you trust may be a ghost","tool":"distributed","text":"03 Processes pause — and the leader you trust may be a ghost A thread can freeze for seconds at any moment: a garbage-collection “stop the world” pause, a VM suspended by the hypervisor, the laptop lid closing. The process can't tell it was paused — it resumes mid-operation believing no time passed.","keywords":["processes","pause","and","the","leader","you","trust","may","be","ghost"]},{"url":"distributed/consistency-consensus.html","page":"Consistency & consensus","section":"Consistency & consensus.","tool":"distributed","text":"Consistency & consensus. Given unreliable networks, lying clocks, and pausing processes, how do a pile of machines agree on anything ? This chapter builds the strongest tools we have: linearizability (behave like a single copy) and consensus (get everyone to agree on one value) — and is honest about","keywords":["consistency","consensus"]},{"url":"distributed/consistency-consensus.html#linearizability","page":"Consistency & consensus","section":"Linearizability — the single-copy illusion","tool":"distributed","text":"01 Linearizability — the single-copy illusion Linearizability is the strongest single-object guarantee: the system behaves as if there is exactly one copy of the data and every operation is atomic. Once one client reads the new value, every later read sees it too — a recency guarantee. In plain Engl","keywords":["linearizability","the","single","copy","illusion"]},{"url":"distributed/consistency-consensus.html#cap","page":"Consistency & consensus","section":"The price: you can't beat a network partition","tool":"distributed","text":"02 The price: you can't beat a network partition The famous CAP theorem is narrow but real: when the network partitions (nodes can't reach each other), a replicated system must choose — stay consistent (refuse requests it can't safely serve) or stay available (answer with possibly-stale data). You c","keywords":["the","price","you","can","beat","network","partition"]},{"url":"distributed/consistency-consensus.html#ordering","page":"Consistency & consensus","section":"Ordering, causality, and total order broadcast","tool":"distributed","text":"03 Ordering, causality, and total order broadcast A lot of consistency reduces to ordering events. The minimum you usually want is causal order : if A caused B (you read a message, then reply), everyone must see A before B. Causal order is the strongest consistency that survives a partition — but it","keywords":["ordering","causality","and","total","order","broadcast"]},{"url":"distributed/consistency-consensus.html#consensus","page":"Consistency & consensus","section":"Consensus — agreeing on one value","tool":"distributed","text":"04 Consensus — agreeing on one value Consensus means several nodes decide on one value and never change their minds, even as some crash. It sounds simple; the FLP result proves it's impossible to guarantee in a purely asynchronous system with even one faulty node — yet real algorithms (Paxos, Raft, ","keywords":["consensus","agreeing","on","one","value"]},{"url":"derived/batch-processing.html","page":"Batch processing","section":"Batch processing.","tool":"derived","text":"Batch processing. Everything so far served live requests. Batch processing is the opposite shape: take a large, fixed input, grind through all of it, and produce a derived output — no user waiting on the other end. It's how search indexes, recommendation models, and reports get built, and its ideas ","keywords":["batch","processing"]},{"url":"derived/batch-processing.html#mindset","page":"Batch processing","section":"The batch mindset","tool":"derived","text":"01 The batch mindset Batch jobs read immutable input and write fresh output, touching nothing in place. That single property is a superpower: a job that fails can just be re-run, the same input always gives the same output, and you can experiment fearlessly because the source data is never harmed. I","keywords":["the","batch","mindset","cat","log","grep","ERROR","sort","uniq"]},{"url":"derived/batch-processing.html#mapreduce","page":"Batch processing","section":"MapReduce — map, shuffle, reduce","tool":"derived","text":"02 MapReduce — map, shuffle, reduce MapReduce runs a job over a distributed filesystem (HDFS) in three movements. Map transforms each input record into key-value pairs. The framework's shuffle sorts and groups every pair by key. Reduce then sees all values for one key together and aggregates them. W","keywords":["mapreduce","map","shuffle","reduce"]},{"url":"derived/batch-processing.html#dataflow","page":"Batch processing","section":"Beyond MapReduce — dataflow engines","tool":"derived","text":"03 Beyond MapReduce — dataflow engines MapReduce writes every intermediate result to disk, and a multi-stage job becomes a chain of separate MapReduce jobs — slow. Modern dataflow engines (Spark, Flink, Tez) model the whole job as one graph of operators and keep intermediate data in memory, recomput","keywords":["beyond","mapreduce","dataflow","engines"]},{"url":"derived/batch-processing.html#derived","page":"Batch processing","section":"The real point: building derived data","tool":"derived","text":"04 The real point: building derived data A batch job's output is derived data — a search index, a recommendation model, a daily report. Because the input is immutable and the transformation is deterministic, the output is a pure function of the input: lose it and you rebuild it; change the code and ","keywords":["the","real","point","building","derived","data"]},{"url":"derived/stream-processing.html","page":"Stream processing","section":"Stream processing.","tool":"derived","text":"Stream processing. Batch processing assumes the input is complete. But data never stops arriving — so stream processing takes batch's ideas and runs them continuously on unbounded input: process each event soon after it happens, forever. It's how derived data stays fresh instead of being a day old.","keywords":["stream","processing"]},{"url":"derived/stream-processing.html#events","page":"Stream processing","section":"Events and streams","tool":"derived","text":"01 Events and streams An event is a small, immutable record that something happened at a point in time. A stream is an unbounded sequence of them. Stream processing is batch processing without the assumption that the input ever ends. In plain English analogy Batch is the cannery processing a fixed p","keywords":["events","and","streams"]},{"url":"derived/stream-processing.html#brokers","page":"Stream processing","section":"Getting events around: two kinds of broker","tool":"derived","text":"02 Getting events around: two kinds of broker Producers and consumers are decoupled by a message broker , and there are two very different designs — the distinction matters more than any other in streaming: Traditional queue (AMQP/JMS) The broker hands each message to one consumer and deletes it aft","keywords":["getting","events","around","two","kinds","of","broker"]},{"url":"derived/stream-processing.html#cdc-windows","page":"Stream processing","section":"Where streams come from, and how you reason about time","tool":"derived","text":"03 Where streams come from, and how you reason about time A powerful source of events is the database itself. Change Data Capture (CDC) turns every write to a system of record into a stream other systems can follow; event sourcing goes further and makes the log of events the source of truth, with cu","keywords":["where","streams","come","from","and","how","you","reason","about","time"]},{"url":"derived/stream-processing.html#joins","page":"Stream processing","section":"Keeping derived data fresh","tool":"derived","text":"04 Keeping derived data fresh The pay-off: a stream processor maintains materialized views that are always up to date. A search index, a cache, a running aggregate, a join of two streams — each is kept current by consuming the event log, so derived data is seconds behind the source instead of a full","keywords":["keeping","derived","data","fresh"]},{"url":"derived/future-data-systems.html","page":"The future of data systems","section":"The future of data systems.","tool":"derived","text":"The future of data systems. No single database does everything well, so real systems are stitched together from many specialised stores. This closing chapter argues that the way to keep them all correct and in sync is dataflow : pick one source of truth, and derive everything else from its stream of","keywords":["the","future","of","data","systems"]},{"url":"derived/future-data-systems.html#no-one-tool","page":"The future of data systems","section":"One source of truth, many views","tool":"derived","text":"01 One source of truth, many views Stop asking one database to be transactional store, search index, cache, and warehouse at once. Instead: keep a single system of record , and treat every other store as a derived view kept up to date from its changelog. Truth lives in one place; everything else is ","keywords":["one","source","of","truth","many","views"]},{"url":"derived/future-data-systems.html#unbundling","page":"The future of data systems","section":"Unbundling the database","tool":"derived","text":"02 Unbundling the database A database already does several jobs internally — a write triggers index updates, replication, and materialized-view maintenance. Kleppmann's idea is to unbundle those into separate tools, composed by a stream that carries every change. Dataflow becomes the integration glu","keywords":["unbundling","the","database"]},{"url":"derived/future-data-systems.html#lambda-kappa","page":"The future of data systems","section":"Lambda or Kappa?","tool":"derived","text":"03 Lambda or Kappa? If derived views come from a stream, what about reprocessing all of history? Two architectures answer differently:","keywords":["lambda","or","kappa"]},{"url":"derived/future-data-systems.html#correctness","page":"The future of data systems","section":"Correctness, and doing right by data","tool":"derived","text":"04 Correctness, and doing right by data Spreading data across many derived stores raises the obvious worry: how do you stay correct without distributed transactions? The answer is the end-to-end argument — build correctness from deterministic derivation plus idempotence and dedup (e.g. a unique requ","keywords":["correctness","and","doing","right","by","data"]},{"url":"reference/decisions.html","page":"Decisions & trade-offs","section":"Decisions & trade-offs.","tool":"reference","text":"Decisions & trade-offs. The recurring data-system decisions on one page — each with a one-line heuristic and the chapter it comes from. Skim it before a design discussion or an interview. This sheet grows as each part ships .","keywords":["decisions","trade","offs"]},{"url":"reference/decisions.html#phase","page":"Decisions & trade-offs","section":"Where the guide is","tool":"reference","text":"01 Where the guide is 🎉 The whole book is covered — chapters 1–12. Foundations, the full distributed-data arc, and derived data (batch, streams, and the future of data systems). Every recurring decision in Designing Data-Intensive Applications now has a one-line heuristic below.","keywords":["where","the","guide","is"]},{"url":"reference/decisions.html#decisions","page":"Decisions & trade-offs","section":"The decisions, so far","tool":"reference","text":"02 The decisions, so far Choosing a data model Self-contained, read together? One-to-many tree (a résumé, order, product) → document . One read, great locality. Interconnected, many ways in? many-to-one / many-to-many → relational . Let the DB do the joins. Densely connected, variable links? Social ","keywords":["the","decisions","so","far","hash","SELECT","FOR","UPDATE"]},{"url":"reference/flashcards.html","page":"Flashcards","section":"Flashcards.","tool":"reference","text":"Flashcards. The key terms and trade-offs of the whole book (Parts I–III) as recall practice. Click a card to flip; mark it Known or Review; Shuffle floats the ones you don't know yet to the front. Progress saves in your browser.","keywords":["flashcards"]},{"url":"reference/flashcards.html#deck","page":"Flashcards","section":"The deck","tool":"reference","text":"01 The deck 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset Fault vs failure A fault is one component deviating from spec; a failure is the whole system stopping. The goal of reliability is to stop faults becoming failures. Known Review The three concerns of a data system Reliability (tolerate faults), scalability (handl","keywords":["the","deck","hash"]}];
